course: dictionary
lesson: 9689
title: Suffixes: word endings that change meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A suffix is a group of letters added to the end of a word. It can make a new word or change how the word is used. Look at «hopeful»: hope plus the suffix -ful makes a word that means full of hope.
explain: The root is the main part of a word, and the suffix goes on the end. Try this sum: «kind» + -ness = «kindness». The suffix -ness turns the word into a name for a quality or state.
explain: Suffixes can also change the job a word does. «She jumped» uses -ed to show the action happened in the past. «Quickly» adds -ly to tell you how something was done.
explain: Some suffixes need a spelling change first. Run becomes running, hope becomes hoping, and happy becomes happiness. Once you know the rules, you can spell words you have never met before.
fact: A suffix is a group of letters added to the end of a word.
fact: «Hopeful» means full of hope, because -ful means full of.
fact: «Careless» uses the suffix -less, which means without.
fact: Suffixes like -ly, -ness and -ment change a word's meaning or job.
fact: In maths and computing, a suffix can be letters or a number added to the end of a name or code.
exercise: In «hopeful», which suffix has been added to hope? | *-ful | -less | -ly | -ness
exercise: What does the suffix -less mean? | a thing | in a certain way | full of | *without
exercise: Which word is made from run plus the suffix -ing? | runs | runner | *running | ran
test: What is the suffix in «careless»? | -ness | care | *-less | -ful
test: Which spelling is correct when you add -ing to hope? | *hoping | hopeing | hopping | hopeign
exam: Which word is made by adding the suffix -ly to quick to mean in a quick way? | quickley | *quickly | quicklly | quickyly
